From: NeoDarwin
Subject: [PATCH] pam_unix: the nullok option

OpenPAM's pam_unix compares crypt(3) of the typed password with pw_passwd,
so an account with an empty password can never authenticate (crypt never
returns ""). NeoDarwin's root has an empty password and logs in on the
console without one (docs/base/session.md, "PAM"). Add FreeBSD pam_unix's
nullok option: with it, an account without a password authenticates without
a prompt, unless the application passes PAM_DISALLOW_NULL_AUTHTOK. Without
it, nothing changes.

--- a/openpam/modules/pam_unix/pam_unix.c
+++ b/openpam/modules/pam_unix/pam_unix.c
@@ -86,6 +86,17 @@
 	if ((pwd = getpwnam(user)) == NULL)
 		return (PAM_USER_UNKNOWN);
 
+	/*
+	 * nullok, as in FreeBSD's pam_unix(8): an account without a password
+	 * authenticates without a prompt, unless the application passes
+	 * PAM_DISALLOW_NULL_AUTHTOK. Without nullok, the comparison below
+	 * refuses it, whatever is typed.
+	 */
+	if (pwd->pw_passwd[0] == '\0' &&
+	    openpam_get_option(pamh, "nullok") != NULL &&
+	    !(flags & PAM_DISALLOW_NULL_AUTHTOK))
+		return (PAM_SUCCESS);
+
 	/* get password */
 #ifndef OPENPAM
 	pam_err = pam_get_item(pamh, PAM_CONV, (const void **)&conv);
